atement is true for the base case $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=2}^{2}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}{{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k+1}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=2}^{n}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)(n+1)$ for all $n\\ge 2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186079012 "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{b-3x}+2, & x\\le 0 \\\\ x+3, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=0$, find the value of the constant $b.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186079015 "Find the value of the constant $a$ that makes the function $g(x)$ below continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2ax+1}, & x\\le 2 \\\\ 2x+5, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186079054 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4, & x<0 \\\\ -x+4, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186082928 "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + 4, \\qquad\na_1 = 2, \\quad\nn \\in\\mathbb{N}.\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\na_n = 4n - 2\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n= \\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid]{\\phantom{k\\geq 1}},$ we have\n\n$$\na_k = \\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid]{\\phantom{4k -2}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\na_{k+1} = \\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid]{\\phantom{4k +2}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $a_n = 4n -2$ for all $n\\in \\mathbb{N}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186082929 "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$