here $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186152718 "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=8-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186152719 "Let $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=6-2x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{-2,0,2\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{-2,0,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186152720 "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2x-9y+11.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186152824 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [#edb/ref 17592186152825 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-6$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [#edb/ref 17592186152827 "![](images/d7/d7db51d6af763e8853f815feb6e74b88d0ca9a19e79c42b3a42da4e8b088f93c.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [#edb/ref 17592186152828 "![](images/e8/e8392135cf2ffbfd91d51fee015cc4ddc6778b890e40e1f63eae18b6927e85e8.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [#edb/ref 17592186157604 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}, & 1<x\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3{x}^{3}}, & 2\\le x<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$"] [#edb/ref 17592186157642 "Prove that if $n$ is odd, then $3n+5$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=\\,${{field-1}} for {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+5 & ={{field-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-4}}. Since $a$ is {{field-5}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-6}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+5 & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $3n+5$ is {{field-8}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186157749 "For the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{4}-2x+1,$ show that $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist {{field-1}} constants $K$ and $\\delta $ such that\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}\\,\\text{for all}\\,0<|x-a|<\\delta .\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le {{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the best upper bound we can form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & <{{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 3$ whenever $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Thus, taking {{field-7}} and {{field-8}} satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f(x)=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186157770 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $4{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186157791 "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}]$"] [#edb/ref 17592186157993 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]≈2.2778,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}, & 0<x<2, \\\\ \\frac{7}{9}, & 2\\le x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186161693 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & -1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\textrm{E}\\bigg[X \\,\\bigg|\\, Y=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\bigg].$"] [#edb/ref 17592186161694 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le x,\\,\\,0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}, & 0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=x\\big]$ for $0 <     x <     2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186161896 "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{3},\\,t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨3{u}^{3},\\,3u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162248 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x},\\, & x>0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162279 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x}, & 1<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162298 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}cxy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le y<1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162299 "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162300 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{x}^{2}y,\\, & {x}^{2}\\le y<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162301 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>Y).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162302 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>\\frac{1}{2},Y>\\frac{1}{2}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162313 "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162314 "Find $\\text{E}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(X+Y)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162346 "For the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162353 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162354 "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [#edb/ref 17592186162355 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [#edb/ref 17592186162356 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -60 \\\\ -60 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162357 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 10 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162358 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162390 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\,0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162391 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,0<x<y,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{5}{8}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162392 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}24xy, & \\,0<x<1,0<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162396 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162404 "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{3}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9{y}^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le {y}^{3},\\,0\\le y\\le 3; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162405 "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}(Y+1)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162406 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}, & 1<x<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162414 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}, & {x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1,\\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(1-{x}^{2}), & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ for $0<x<1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162417 "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ is shown above."] [#edb/ref 17592186162425 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=20,$ where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,20.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162429 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x>0,\\,y>0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162433 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=20$ from a population where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,20.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186162588 "Consider the bilinear form $B(x,y),$ given by \n\n$$\nB(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate $B(x,y)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162600 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162607 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n2{y}_{1}^{2}-\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{2}^{2},\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?"] [#edb/ref 17592186162609 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2},\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2} &=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{3} &={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?"] [#edb/ref 17592186162620 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?"] [#edb/ref 17592186162641 "Find the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162644 "Find the $30$th percentile of the random variable $Y$ if the probability distribution function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162651 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{128}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}), & -2\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162663 "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162664 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162666 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186162720 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=5.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ find the principal axes of the curve."] [#edb/ref 17592186166505 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=7{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166506 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations given by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-6{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166510 "![](images/3f/3fa8db8d4186569020d5805ac76eee4f92f1c261dd2acb2502409a9b2b6db56b.svg)\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math]}} &=-3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ \\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math]}} &=\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{XXXXXXXX}[/math]}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t),$ respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186166511 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = x^3,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166518 "Suppose we wish to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) =c,\n$$\n\nand we take a step of size $\\Delta x$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Fill in the blanks to complete the weighted-average formula for the increment and the stage definitions, starting at the point $(x,y) .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & =f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})\\,\\text{ where }\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{(q_0,1,L)}[/math]}},\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & =f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})\\,\\text{ where }\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{(q_0,1,L)}[/math]}}) \\\\ {y}_{3}^{′} & =f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})\\,\\text{ where }\\,({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{(q_0,1,L)}[/math]}},\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166523 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+4{t}^{2}+t-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)-{t}^{2}+5t+6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWrite the system of linear ODEs in matrix form."] [#edb/ref 17592186166534 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space."] [#edb/ref 17592186166536 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/04/040ac76a28687db1c99d3379242b726a85aa4baf269a7c6d35d9662af1d3bd78.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186166537 "![](images/25/25d4f77a3f596202229581e06d88a9e3bdd84dc6b8b7e14b6f46b3b3d2f125d2.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\nI. $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=6y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nII. $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=6y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nIII. $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nIV. $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166538 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = -\\sin x, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos x,$ compute the local truncation error $\\tau_{6}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places."] [#edb/ref 17592186166540 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nInterpret the stability of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the equilibrium point (the origin) for the given decoupled system of linear differential equations."] [#edb/ref 17592186166542 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/47/470210fb9509e87c3e51a990bac7483e5862d2f37afdce9823279838e8391592.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186166543 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=9x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-6x(t)-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 2]^T,$ respectively. Complete the following statements about the system by filling in the blanks.\n\nIdentify stable and unstable lines, and classify the equilibrium point (origin)."] [#edb/ref 17592186166545 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-8x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-8y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSketch the phase portrait of the linear system above."] [#edb/ref 17592186166546 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $\\mathbf{v} = [1, \\ -1]^T$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [0, \\ 1]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ Sketch the phase portrait of the linear system above."] [#edb/ref 17592186166547 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system."] [#edb/ref 17592186166548 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=4x+5y-z,\\, && y(0)=3 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=5x-4y+2z,\\, && z(0)=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166549 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=x-y+2z,\\, && y(1)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=3x+y-z,\\, && z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3)$ and $z(3).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166550 "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](images/2b/2bc77eafe7c4362a1020c1f547a19ef0e72c309ebb82d1480e2ffec5bdfd8b38.svg)\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above."] [#edb/ref 17592186166552 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda=\\pm2\\text{i}.$ Provide an interpretation of the equilibrium point (origin) for the system of differential equations given above."] [#edb/ref 17592186166553 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x), w=w(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=2y+z+x,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=y+z+2x,\\, && z(0)=0 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=wy-x,\\, && w(0)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nApproximate values of $y$, $z$ and $w$ at $x = 1$ using Euler's method if the step size is $\\Delta x = 1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166554 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=-4x+7xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=9y-5xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model."] [#edb/ref 17592186166555 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=-0.4x+0.4xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=y-4xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.25, \\, 1]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point."] [#edb/ref 17592186166556 "Consider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=-10x+5xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=21y-7xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t), y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nDescribe the trajectory and the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n![](images/86/86002a25d2c60e07c34976541a1c050e606d20e4ffa7928a44f940bf5fa736e3.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186166557 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=-8x+2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=4y-xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Provide an interpretation of each equation in the model."] [#edb/ref 17592186166558 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=5x(1-0.2x)-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the equilibria of the model."] [#edb/ref 17592186166559 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=5x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-y+2xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 2.5]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point."] [#edb/ref 17592186166560 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=4x(1-2x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 0]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point."] [#edb/ref 17592186166561 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=6x(1-0.1x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-7y+5xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators."] [#edb/ref 17592186166677 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}⟨0,t,4⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}⟨0,4,-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186166678 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point $t=\\pi$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩, \\\\ N(\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186166679 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,2,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ denotes the unit tangent vector."] [#edb/ref 17592186166697 "The disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup A_3$ of events $A_1,A_2,A_3$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P(A_1|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.23, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.26, & P({A}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.18, & P({A}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186166703 "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\ny''' - 3\\sqrt{t}\\, y'' + t^3 y' - 4t^2 y= 0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n