in}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167307 "$$\n{A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Find the matrix $C$ given that $B=\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $B$ are $[C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167314 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\sqrt{3}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167330 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167426 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167463 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186167496 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186167497 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=2$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,-1,2⟩, \\\\ N(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186167498 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector."] [#edb/ref 17592186167543 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩, \\\\ N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186167544 "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector."] [#edb/ref 17592186171717 "Suppose we want to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=2xz+y^2$ on the intersection of the surfaces $x-z=2$ and $x^2+y^2+2z^2=3$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2z,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2x⟩ &