$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186180273 "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=10-2x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186180274 "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186180276 "Let $R=[2,5]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186184065 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\textrm A1] - [\\textrm A8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{w}$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add [math]-\\mathbf{w}[/math] to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186184751 "An engineer measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each road bridge in a city. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular bridge. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.669 \\\\ 0.743\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.743 \\\\ -0.669\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the engineer wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?"] [#edb/ref 17592186184762 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*$\\alpha 0=0$ for any $\\alpha \\in R$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha (0+0) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =(\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0)+(-\\alpha 0) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-\\alpha 0\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =\\alpha 0+(\\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186184764 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $w=0.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186184775 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186184778 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186184854 "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places."] [#edb/ref 17592186184879 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186188023 "Consider the polar curve $r=\\cot^2\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in \\left(0, \\pi\\right).$ Given that \n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta(\\cos\\theta + 2\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta), \\qquad\ny'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta(1+\\sin^2\\theta),\n$$\n\nand $y'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right) = 0$\n\nII. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=-\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cs