lign=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [#edb/ref 17592186208957 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [#edb/ref 17592186209278 "![](images/63/63bfb71511b0debb4b1f1c3d093fb4b84f38aebdf0322290314e89217e76b648.png)\n\nIn the $xy$-plane shown, square $ABCD$ has its diagonals aligned along the $x$- and $y$-axes. What is the area, in square units, of the square?"] [#edb/ref 17592186209279 "![](images/c7/c79f8cf904878714a78a2edad0baf08d6f507f26bd73abb2efeb5b12e12cadca.png)\n\nIn the $xy$-plane shown, rhombus $EFGH$ has its diagonals aligned along the $x$- and $y$-axes. What is the area, in square units, of the rhombus?"] [#edb/ref 17592186221148 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-4{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in\\mathbb R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function ![](images/74/74e10d18890ebd27b2339e13ac2bbc9e0821c1dfea752478a90877d0a0059ff6.png) is a solution of the system.\n\n- The function ![](images/f0/f08f12db6684778c1f5734c0731a370663fcfb23cd958dde49e3a2f41cd0e50a.png) is a solution of the system.\n\n- The function ![](images/f5/f5c546b729e994f79408b4344979446aac8aa3820c1798251bc50378f8f7159b.png) is a solution of the system.\n\nAlso, determine which pair of solutions forms a set of fundamental solutions to the system."] [#edb/ref 17592186221166 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [#edb/ref 17592186221167 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186221169 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=-5,$ with corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [3, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-2, \\ 1]^T,$ respectively. Using this information, sketch the phase portrait of the system."] [#edb/ref 17592186221170 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$  Sketch the phase portrait of the system."] [#edb/ref 17592186221171 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above."] [#edb/ref 17592186221174 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above."] [#edb/ref 17592186221181 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4x(t)+4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast=[2, \\ 0]^T?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186221182 "Given the system of differential equations, determine whether the system is almost linear at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast= [0, \\: 0]^T.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-{y}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{x(t)} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-{x}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{y(t)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186221188 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-2y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?"] [#edb/ref 17592186221189 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T.$ What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [#edb/ref 17592186221923 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-6{x}_{1}(t)+7{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below. $\\,$ The function $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}7{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186221931 "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186221941 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [#edb/ref 17592186221944 "Suppose we wish to find $L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}$ given $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{{field-1}}\\,dτ=A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}=L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={{field-3}} \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & ={{field-4}}(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt={{field-5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186221951 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186221952 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186221953 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186221960