 }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186222042 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=5t+3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186222044 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186228904 "For the initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation $y'=-\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0, \\lambda = 12,$ and step size $\\Delta x?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2.$\n\nII. The stability region is $\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac16\\right)^2\\right| < 1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186228910 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-7x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to the system of ODEs above.\n\n*Hint: ![](images/4c/4c7ca8aa3fb83e7c4ce806080257e8f3b3d5186e19f10649a0bed7202f6501b6.svg) where ![](images/04/0492dd33f8d39d4a74139be25415d6d943888d8ccbf67158690f0960c5334415.svg)*"] [#edb/ref 17592186228916 "![](images/3d/3db7e4a714d16b8f1