- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |  |  |  |  |"] [#edb/ref 17592186305064 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186305068 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(-1,2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?"] [#edb/ref 17592186305069 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(-1,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?"] [#edb/ref 17592186305070 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(3,-1)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?"] [#edb/ref 17592186305071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(0,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?"] [#edb/ref 17592186305075 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186305077 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186305079 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186305081 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186305082 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186305084 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186305093 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186305119 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+5 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{s+5}{s(s+1)},\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-1}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186305121 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+3{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}} &={{field-2}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1 &=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186305124 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+4 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)-2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}} &={X}_{1}(s)+{{field-2}} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1 &=-{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186305125 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}} &={{field-2}} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-2 &=-{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186305126 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/f1/f1335e5420fe6e475cded1092e9b1ba621e0d88f46110b69ffef5e35ed080