ions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-6}} \\\\ y &={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/39/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6dd454fbe71ba953b05c4da22d8c36f76277a.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308444 "Find $(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308449 "Consider the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-y,\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nSuppose we approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ compute the local truncation error ${τ}_{6}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $Δx=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{6}≈$\n{{field-1}}"] [#edb/ref 17592186308456 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=5x,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308457 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308458 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308469 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[3,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6ec9eac46644188b8b41a1a7ea28ca0542ee7e5c69d713028481904e90dd557a.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-6}} \\\\ y &=3(t+1){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.20$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.20$ | $3$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/b0/b0be26a1544bbb3117afab15d004bafee70d831fffab4b1f6921870871ac4768.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/64/64e685f4ea06a8f497ddfbe50a092a0362b8c92a9d2f78f9da2a8164113a785a.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308471 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-5x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/55/558ff2994d715139bde11747fdffddbbbd20f17f80c8deb3b90f4ae76078bdba.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-6}} \\\\ y &=(2t+1){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.45$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/10/10050fff096c999bdd1d845a247dac87a3135458313338f62018612639a011b0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c2/c227e135e04b76f093d8c2ad6e2a4365e206c19b281f1a3df7a4b65dd479f411.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186308476 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode."] [#edb/ref 17592186308477 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of multiplici