)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [#edb/ref 17592186311594 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [#edb/ref 17592186311602 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+2y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186311604 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)-6x(t)+2y(t)+9 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+y(t)-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[3,\\,0]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [#edb/ref 17592186311613 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186311615 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186311616 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)-2y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186311617 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={y}^{3}(t)+x(t)-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186311649 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-2,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4+3t \\\\ y &=1-4t \\\\ z &=-3-2t\\end{aligned}\\right..$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,-4y\\,-\\,$ \n{{field-2}} $z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$\\,=0$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311650 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,4,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1+2t \\\\ y &=5+3t \\\\ z &=7-t\\end{aligned}\\right..$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,-\\,z\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311665 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,1,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ y &=-2+4t \\\\ z &=3+t\\end{aligned}\\right..$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311718 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-4,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2+t \\\\ y &=1+4t \\\\ z &=5\\end{aligned}\\right..$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311877 "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={{field-2}}+{{field-3}} \\\\  & +2ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186311880 "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+y\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={{field-2}}+{{field-3}} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186311915 "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)=y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={{field-2}}-2\\cdot {{field-3}} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186311934 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311937 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16y=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-4x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311938 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311939 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y