e{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186402002 "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186402009 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [#edb/ref 17592186402016 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous at $t=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,7t+6,\\,3-{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨1,\\,{t}^{2/3},\\,t⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt[3]{t},\\,2t+3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186402067 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-4{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$*"] [#edb/ref 17592186402074 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$*"] [#edb/ref 17592186402113 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [#edb/ref 17592186402154 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186402161 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?"] [#edb/ref 17592186402168 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=2x(1-2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-y+xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n$$J(x^{\\ast})=\\begin{bmatrix}{{field-1}}&{{field-2}}\\\\{{field-3}}&{{field-4}}\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186402302 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-2,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-2)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186402320 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 2, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186402338 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ -2, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186402434 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*$0x=0$ for any $x\\in V$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (0+0)x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{property of number }0\\,] \\\\ 0x+0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0x+(-0x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-0x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x+(0x+(-0x)) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0x+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186402495 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186402602 "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & x+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{2}+0x+16} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{2}+4x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4x-16}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,32\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186402662 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{3}, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{6}+1, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [#edb/ref 17592186402756 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z &=2 \\\\ y-z &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-J