he inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & ={{field-12}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true for all {{field-15}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186418038 "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n${\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & ={{field-12}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {5}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true for all {{field-15}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186418387 "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=3}^{3}(2i+3)={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3)={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3 & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ is true for all {{field-9}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186418388 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}2(5i-3)={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}2(5i-3)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}2(5i-3)={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186418729 "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n}-{3}^{n},\\,{a}_{1}=7,\\,n\\in N,\n$$\n\nuse mathematical induction to prove the following statement: \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side of our statement, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}={{field-4}