.\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is {{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186421865 "Compute $P(X<-5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{4x},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186421866 "Compute $P(X>3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186422153 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{5t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/2a/2ab8c69672acf64e93615960fdbb34445d4f81617cae16f90a8e4f30252607ba.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/d1/d1b7ffd22ad77fe94a994659fb7a091692b23bafb3012a705e964419fb71e8aa.png)\n\nFinally, since the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-9}},\nsolutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector\n{{field-10}} \nas they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7a/7a87773278ef8c921a9e7002a376bac1de99e969c2eb10e4a6f104a854094469.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186422154 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that 