441149 "Let $⟨-12,y⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x+9y &=42 \\\\ kx+10y &=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. $|\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ k & 10\\end{aligned}|=-12$\n\nII. $|\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ k & 10\\end{aligned}|=-38$\n\nIII. $|\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ k & 10\\end{aligned}|=456$"] [#edb/ref 17592186441315 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=3{e}^{t}+4{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}$  for $s>1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186441316 "Consider a function $z(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{z(t)\\}=Z(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186441317 "Consider a function $n(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{n(t)\\}=N(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186441318 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=t({e}^{2t}-1)$ for $s>2.$"] [#edb/r