,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{3}+3y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186591641 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\,{x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y\\le 1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186591650 "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{3}⁡(\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )({cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta )),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186591652 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-2)}$ ,$\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-1}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186591654 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)+5,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{1}{s},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s-4}-\\frac{2}{s}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [#edb/ref 17592186591656 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ g(y), & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186591663 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(-3<Y<1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186591671 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, 