^{2}}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ g(x), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186593217 "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?"] [#edb/ref 17592186593220 "Prove that $11n+3$ is odd provided that $n$ is an even integer.\n\nLet $n$ be an even integer. Then, $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n+3 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}.  Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n+3 & ={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $11n+3$ is {{field-7}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186593228 "Prove that if $n$ is an odd integer, then ${n}^{2}-3$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}-3$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-3 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}-3={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{2}-3$ is {{field-7}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186593251 "Prove that ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ is odd if $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}-2n+1 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{3}-2n+1={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{3}-2n+1$ is {{field-7}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186593253 "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?"] [#edb/ref 17592186593256 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=e-1,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186593274 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{12},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}, & 0<x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}, & 1\\le x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186593275 "Prove that  $3n-1$ is even whenever $n$ is odd.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=\\,${{field-1}} for {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-1 & ={{field-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-4}}. Since $a$ is {{field-5}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-6}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-1 & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $3n-1$ is {{field-8}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186593306 "Prove that $3n+4$ is even provided that $n$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is even, then $n=\\,${{field-1}} for {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+4 & ={{field-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-4}}. Since $a$ is {{field-5}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-6}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+4 & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $3n+4$ is {{field-8}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186594147 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(X<\\frac{2}{3}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186594150 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$7n-2$ is odd for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}} \n$7n-2$ is {{field-4}}.\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n-2 & =7({{field-6}})-2 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}}. \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."] [#edb/ref 17592186594151 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{11}{18},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2582,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2664,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [#edb/ref 17592186594154 "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}} &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})$ is true for all {{field-9}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186594155 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x-y}, & \\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186594158 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{n+1}-1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=0}^{0}{3}^{i} &=\\frac{1}{2}({{field-1}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({{field-3}}).\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({{field-4}}).\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{3}^{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{n+1}-1)$ for all $n\\ge 0.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186594164 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,0<x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{2}\\,x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{2}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186594181 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}n(3n+1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}(3i-1) &=\\frac{1}{2}{{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(3i-1)=\\frac{1}{2}n(3n+1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186594182 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{8}, & \\,0\\le x<2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x<2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186594191 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy+1}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186594193 "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{2}}, & 0\\le x<1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2})}{2}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n