fficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [#edb/ref 17592186594226 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$4∣{n}^{3}+4$ for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n4{{field-4}}{n}^{3}+4.\n$$\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}+4 & =({{field-6}})^{3}+4 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}} by $4.$ \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."] [#edb/ref 17592186594227 "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}} &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ is true for all {{field-9}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186594229 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n