ction $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186595931 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{y+1}{3}$ for $y\\in [0,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [#edb/ref 17592186595933 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{10}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{30}$ for $y\\in [0,3]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [#edb/ref 17592186595936 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(x+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186595937 "Let $n$ be an integer. Using the contrapositive, prove that $n$ is odd provided that $7n+6$ is odd.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nIf $n$ is {{field-3}}, then $n=$ {{field-4}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+6 & ={{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-6}}. Since $a$ is {{field-7}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+6 & ={{field-9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich {{field-10}} a multiple of $2.$ Hence, $7n+6$ is {{field-11}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186595940 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡9, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,0\\le Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡9).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186595941 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(y+2), & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186595947 "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${5}^{2n-1}+3$ is divisible by $4$ for all $n\\in N.$\n\nLet $P(n)$ denote the following sequence of statements:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,4\\,∣\\,{5}^{2n-1}+3\n$$\n\n We proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nSubstituting $n=\\,${{field-2}} into the expression ${5}^{2n-1}+3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-3}} &={{field-4}},\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=\\,${{field-7}}.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-8}}, we have that\n\n$$\nP(k):\\,{{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}+{5}^{2k-1}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-12}} by the inductive hypothesis, and {{field-13}} we have that {{field-14}}, \nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $4\\,∣\\,{5}^{2n-1}+3$ for all {{field-15}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186595950 "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}-{t}^{-3/2}{y}^{′}-{t}^{-1}y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} &={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} &={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′} &=f(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186595962 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{3}{2},$ find $\\text{E}[2Y-4X].$"] [#edb/ref 17592186595965 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}x+1$ for $y\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [#edb/ref 17592186595966 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x{y}^{2}+2}{6}, & 2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186595969 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x$ for $x\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [#edb/ref 17592186595970 "Let $n$ be an integer. Prove that $n$ is odd provided that ${n}^{2}-5n+5$ is even.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}\n$$\n\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nIf $n$ is {{field-3}}, then $n=$ {{field-4}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-5n+5 & ={{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-6}}. Since $a$ is {{field-7}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-5n+5 & ={{field-9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich {{field-10}} a multiple of $2.$ Therefore ${n}^{2}-5n+5$ is {{field-11}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186595971 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{4}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186595983 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ for $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [#edb/ref 17592186595984 "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${n}^{2}+n$ is even for all $n