d $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186595990 "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}-7y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} &={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} &=f(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186595993 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}({x}^{2}+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186595994 "Let $n$ be an integer. Using the contrapositive, prove that $3n+8$ is odd implies $n$ is odd.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nIf $n$ is {{field-3}}, then $n=$ {{field-4}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+8 & ={{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-6}}. Since $a$ is {{field-7}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+8 & ={{field-9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich {{field-10}} a multiple of $2.$ Hence, $3n+8$ is {{field-11}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186595996 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186595998 "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+7y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} &={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} &=f(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186596621 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=2xz+y,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{x-y}{{z}^{2}},\\, && z(0)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}"] [#edb/ref 17592186596623 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\ x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186596628 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡1 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186596646 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=3x-y+z,\\, && y(1)=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=2x-3y-z,\\, && z(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{7}{2})$ and $z\\,(\\frac{7}{2}).$\n\n$\\,y\\,(\\frac{7}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z\\,(\\frac{7}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}}"] [#edb/ref 17592186596647 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to th