ding to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $210?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ x≡13 & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡9 & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ x≡11 & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186596723 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186597540 "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=7{a}_{n}-6{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=5,\\,{a}_{2}=35,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2\n$$\n\nuse strong induction to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={6}^{n}-1,\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-8}} for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={6}^{n}-1$ for all {{field-9}} for some {{field-10}}. In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={{field-11}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: Using the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =({{field-13}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{a}_{k}-6{a}_{k-1} & =7({{field-14}})-6({{field-15}}) \\\\  & =6({{field-16}})-{{field-17}} \\\\  & ={{field-18}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={6}^{n}-1$ is true for all {{field-19}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186597559 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-1},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=-\\frac{1}{s-1}+\\frac{4}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{3t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [#edb/ref 17592186597561 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{2} & =2{c}_{3}+{c}_{2}, \\\\ {b}_{3} & =-{c}_{3}+2{c}_{1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & a & b \\\\ -1 & 2 & c\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186597567 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) 