b/ref 17592186598565 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}xy, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $y>2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186598569 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}(x-y), & 2\\le x\\le 5,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<2,\\,y<3.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186598571 "![](images/c6/c64cfa701997c2b9e5e54f7eb77afb9927b878065af9c32fed86f09f140a4ec4.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{2}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186598582 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x\\sqrt{y}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>2,\\,y>1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186598595 "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n${\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{n} & {2}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & ={{field-12}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{n} & {2}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true for all {{field-15}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186598611 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}x{e}^{y}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186598619 "![](images/3c/3c45364ee569d9ee24bbac167ad2a76f8a0a1a519f6604b7f9427d723c59abab.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6y{e}^{-3x}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{4}{3}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186598632 "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & ={{field-12}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true for all {{field-15}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186598633 "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+\\frac{1}{t}\\,{y}^{″}-{t}^{2}{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} &={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} &={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′} &=f(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186599196 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x+1$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{4}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186599232 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{5}{9},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186599237 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-5 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}} &={X}_{1}(s)-\\frac{5}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s) &={{field-2}}+{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186599256 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}y({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=0$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{2}{3},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186599262 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+5{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1 &={{field-1}} \\\\ {{field-2}} &=-2{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [#edb/ref 17592186599790 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<-1\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}+1){y}^{2}, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,y>1 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>1,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186599795 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=2-3y+z,\\, && y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=x+2y,\\, && z(0)=0 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=y-2w,\\, && w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-6}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186599797 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)-4x(t)+y(t)+4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,0]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [#edb/ref 17592186599799 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3-x, & x<1 \\\\ 2x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186599805 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)({e}^{y}-1)}{x{e}^{y}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 0.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186599806 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+5x(t)+2y(t)+6 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)+6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[-2,\\,0]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [#edb/ref 17592186599815 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=4-y-2z,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=x+w,\\, && z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=y+z,\\, && w(0)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-6}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186599834 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x({y}^{2}-1)}{{y}^{2}(x+3)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{X}(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $x\\ge 1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186599835 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ x, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ y, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & y>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186599842 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent rand