</td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5871\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5910\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5948\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6293\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6331\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [#edb/ref 17592186604007 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186604043 "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${2}^{n-1}\\le n!$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}} &={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\\le {{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-9}} & ={{field-10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {2}^{k-1} & \\le {{field-11}} \\\\  & \\le {{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${2}^{n-1}\\le n!$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186604050 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{19}{12},$  $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{5}{12},$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=\\frac{1}{4},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186604061 "Use mathematical induction to prove that $2{n}^{2}>(n+1)^{2}$ for $n\\ge 3,n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}} &={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(k+1)^{2} & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+4k+2 & {{field-10}}{{field-11}} \\\\  & >{{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $2{n}^{2}>(n+1)^{2}$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186604062 "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${n}^{2}>2n-2$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}} &={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2} & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+2k+1 & {{field-10}}{{field-11}} \\\\  & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & >{{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{2}>2n-2$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186604063 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & \\,0<x<1,0<y<x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{8}<Y<\\frac{1}{6}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186604072 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2s-1}{{s}^{2}-2s+2},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=${{field-1}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186604573 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=90$ from a population where $10%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,90.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{90}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [#edb/ref 17592186604582 "There are $30$ students in the $6$th grade of Arizona Elementary School. From this population of students, $20%$ have blue eyes. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has blue eyes} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,30.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186604586 "For the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-y &=6 \\\\ 5y-2x &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits representation using matrices is\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td>\n</td><td>\n</td><td>\n</td><td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$y$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td>$=$</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n."] [#edb/ref 17592186604604 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=230,$ where $40%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,230.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186604607 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=120,$ where $35%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,120.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=1).$"] 