n\\begin{aligned} & >{{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${5}^{n}\\ge 4n+1$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186612408 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x+7y+z &=1 \\\\ 5x+2y-5z &=0 \\\\ 11x+3y+z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 7 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & b \\\\ 11 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ c \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186612412 "Use mathematical induction to prove that $2{n}^{2}+1\\le 3{n}^{3}$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}} &={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\\le {{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(k+1)^{2}+1 & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+1+4k+2 & \\le {{field-10}} \\\\  & \\le {{field-11}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={{field-12}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $2{n}^{2}+1\\le 3{n}^{3}$ is true for all\n{{field-13}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186612415 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+z &=6 \\\\ 4x+7y+2z &=0 \\\\ 3x+y+z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & b & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186612424 "Which of the diagrams shows the solution of the following system?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge x+1 \\\\ y<3x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186612436 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3z-6x-4 &=0 \\\\ 2y-x-3z-1 &=0 \\\\ 5y+2x-9 &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n|  |  | $-6$ | {{field-1}} | $3$ |  |  |  | $x$ |  |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $-1$ | $2$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |  | $y$ |  | $=$ |  | $1$ |  | . |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | $5$ | $0$ |  |  |  | $z$ |  |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [#edb/ref 17592186612448 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4z+2x+y-1 &=0 \\\\ y+2z+3x &=2 \\\\ x+5z+2y-3 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n|  |  | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $4$ |  |  |  | $x$ |  |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  | $y$ |  | $=$ |  | $2$ |  | . |\n|  |  | $1$ | $2$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  | $z$ |  |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [#edb/ref 17592186612454 "Which of the diagrams shows the solution of the following system?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y<2 \\\\ y\\le -x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186612471 "Use mathematical induction to prove that $4n<{n}^{2}-7$ for $n\\ge 6,n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}} &={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=6.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}<{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(k+1) & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k+4 & {{field-10}}{{field-11}} \\\\  & <{{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $4n<{n}^{2}-7$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186613153 "Given that \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\,,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ -1\\,,\\, & x>1\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186613154 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [#edb/ref 17592186613164 "Let $D=[0,1]\\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-xy}{3},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-y}{4},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$"] [#edb/ref 17592186613202 "Given that \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\,,\\, & x<-1 \\\\ -1\\,,\\, & -1\\le x<1 \\\\ 2\\,,\\, & x\\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186613751 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186613762 "The solution of the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-5 &=0 \\\\ 5x+3y-10 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ngives the point where the dirt tracks meet. This solution can be written in matrix form as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the values of $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,c\\,=$\n{{field-3}}"] [#edb/ref 17592186613800 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $X.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186614589 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}(5-x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<2 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186614624 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]≈1.0265,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & 1\\le x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186614641 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 5 & -10 \\\\ a & -10 & c\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [#edb/ref 17592186615380 "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}, & x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$"] [#edb/ref 17592186615453 "Mary wonders where and when they'd met if Sam left home $3.8$ minutes earlier and walked with a speed of $50$ meters per minute. She finds that the distance $d$ from home they'd meet can be modeled using the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}d+60t &=960 \\\\ d-50t &=190.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time (in minutes) since Mary left the community park.\n\nMary and Sam would meet$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$minutes after Mary left the community park.\n\nMary and Sam would meet at a distance of $\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,$meters from home."] [#edb/ref 17592186616139 "Consider two sets of $20$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{20},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{20}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{x}_{i}=196, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{y}_{i}=204, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=2\\,079.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, ${S}_{xy}=$\n{{field-1}}."] [#edb/ref 17592186616177 "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu$ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186616199 "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu$ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y=2{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(10<Y<24).$ \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *