ystem above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $xyz?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186619724 "Solve the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=0 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{2}-\\frac{y}{2} &=\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186620953 "![](images/b5/b50265f0a2c46366b3ebba823c69e38b4f374dc8c5f28c38143aa027fdfcd482.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - y &=0 \\\\ 2x - y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$."] [#edb/ref 17592186620957 "![](images/0c/0c71cbd7304e96798a96b737af8e78cd227f43053f50bf5ccb0346bfe3121ecd.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2x + 6 \\\\ 3x - y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$."] [#edb/ref 17592186620962 "![](images/f7/f7a0a2bf71c0c41e0a6519e9d86ed483a54312557a4d6b2aca0a7d74ce3d9cb0.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=10 \\\\ x - y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$."] [#edb/ref 17592186620963 "![](images/71/710492e1cd9bb9f722a15cdd30fa19bcd803dbc2542bf0f681b06f2fd8602f26.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x + y &=-3 \\\\ y &=4x + 17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$."] [#edb/ref 17592186620974 "![](images/65/65b4d014c48a40f46c7d79b6c56e7fa66e8dda674d6e68be8be61bd62301fae8.png)\n\nConsider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-5x + 16 \\\\ x + y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the diagram provided to determine the solution $(x, y)$ of this system.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$."] [#edb/ref 17592186622766 "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}, & x=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}, & x=3\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}, & x=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}, & x=3\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=2\\end{aligned}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186622783 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}(y-1)}{y}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>1,\\,y\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186622811 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{1-x}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186622818 "Which of the following functions are *not* valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}, & x=2\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{7}, & x=2\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}, & x=2\\end{aligned}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186623621 "Two different call centers recorded the number of calls handled each hour. The data for both centers is shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Call Center A}: & \\,18,\\,18,\\,19,\\,19,\\,19,\\,19,\\,20,\\,20 \\\\ \\text{Call Center B}: & \\,3,\\,7,\\,10,\\,18,\\,25,\\,27,\\,28,\\,32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nSince the dataset corresponding to \n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the mean absolute deviation (MAD) of Call Center A is \n{{field-2}} \nthan the MAD of Call Center B."] [#edb/ref 17592186623626 "A retailer compared the daily sales (hundreds of dollars) at two stores. The sales amounts for each store are given below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Store A}: & \\,18,\\,18,\\,19,\\,19,\\,20,\\,20,\\,21,\\,21 \\\\ \\text{Store B}: & \\,5,\\,8,\\,12,\\,15,\\,25,\\,28,\\,30,\\,35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nSince the dataset corresponding to \n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the standard deviation of Store A is \n{{field-2}} \nthan the standard deviation of Store B."] [#edb/ref 17592186623631 "Consider the following datasets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A: & \\,3,\\,3,\\,3,\\,4,\\,4 \\\\ B: & \\,12,\\,12,\\,12,\\,12,\\,12 \\\\ C: & \\,0,\\,2,\\,5,\\,7,\\,11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nList the datasets in order of their standard deviations, from **smallest to largest**."] [#edb/ref 17592186623637 "Consider the following datasets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A: & \\,95,\\,95,\\,96,\\,96,\\,96 \\\\ B: & \\,100,\\,100,\\,100,\\,100,\\,100 \\\\ C: & \\,60,\\,80,\\,100,\\,120,\\,160\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nList the datasets in order of their mean absolute deviation (MAD), from **smallest to largest**."] [#edb/ref 17592186623645 "Two different software teams recorded the number of tickets resolved each day. The data for both teams is shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Team A}: & \\,12,\\,12,\\,13,\\,13,\\,13,\\,14,\\,14,\\,14 \\\\ \\text{Team B}: & \\,3,\\,5,\\,7,\\,12,\\,20,\\,22,\\,25,\\,27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nSince the dataset corresponding to \n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the mean absolute deviation (MAD) of Team A is \n{{field-2}} \nthan the MAD of Team B."] [#edb/ref 17592186623647 "A project manager recorded the completion times, in days, for tasks handled by two teams. The completion times are given below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Team A}: & \\,5,\\,8,\\,9,\\,12,\\,15,\\,19,\\,22,\\,25 \\\\ \\text{Team B}: & \\,14,\\,14,\\,15,\\,15,\\,16,\\,16,\\,17,\\,17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nSince the dataset corresponding to \n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the standard deviation of Team A is \n{{field-2}} \nthan the standard deviation of Team B."] [#edb/ref 17592186623653 "Consider the following datasets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A: & \\,12,\\,12,\\,12,\\,12,\\,12 \\\\ B: & \\,8,\\,8,\\,9,\\,9,\\,9 \\\\ C: & \\,2,\\,6,\\,10,\\,14,\\,18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nList the datasets in order of their standard deviations, from **smallest to largest**."] [#edb/ref 17592186623666 "Two warehouses recorded the delivery delays, in minutes, for eight recent deliveries each. The delays for individual deliveries are listed below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Warehouse A}: & \\,2,\\,3,\\,5,\\,7,\\,8,\\,12,\\,15,\\,18 \\\\ \\text{Warehouse B}: & \\,8,\\,8,\\,9,\\,9,\\,10,\\,10,\\,11,\\,11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nSince the dataset corresponding to \n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the standard deviation of Warehouse A is \n{{field-2}} \nthan the standard deviation of Warehouse B."] [#edb/ref 17592186623681 "Consider the following datasets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A: & \\,48,\\,48,\\,49,\\,49,\\,49 \\\\ B: & \\,30,\\,40,\\,60,\\,80,\\,90 \\\\ C: & \\,50,\\,50,\\,50,\\,50,\\,50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nList the datasets in order of their mean absolute deviation (MAD), from **smallest to largest**."] [#edb/ref 175921